#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Manuscript figures with GCM ensemble uncertainty.

Reviewer-facing changes in this version:
- Figure 1 map coastlines are thicker and darker.
- Figure 1b uses the exact same six realm-level per-GCM net changes as
  Figure 3. Positive realm net changes are summed as gain, negative realm
  net changes are summed in absolute value as loss, and global net change
  is their difference. Bars/marker show five-GCM means and whiskers show
  five-GCM min-max ranges.
- Figure 5 duplicates Figure 1's four-panel layout but panel b shows only
  the realm-consistent global net change as a bar with five-GCM min-max
  whiskers.
- Figure 1d reports percentages for every loss/gain category, including
  small land-use gains.
- Figure 2 saves the global and six-realm time series as separate figures,
  preserving the realm-panel aspect ratio and explicitly joining 2014 to 2015.
- Figure 3 separates total GDW and GDW without land-use impact and reports
  five-GCM min-max ranges.
- Figure 4 uses clearer coastlines and hatches only biome–realm units
  where the five GCMs disagree on the sign of change.

- Figure 3 uses four wide lollipop panels, displays the six realm labels once,
  excludes the global aggregate, reports net change, and shows five-GCM mean
  and min–max spread with corrected land-use wording.

Earlier structural changes retained:
- Fig 3: REDESIGNED from a single row of 4 scenario panels (each overlaying
  two marker types -- Total GDW and GDW-without-landuse -- with dual GCM
  range lines per realm) to a 2-row x 4-column small-multiple grid: row 1
  is Total GDW, row 2 is GDW without landuse impact, one marker type and
  one GCM range per subplot. The overlay design was hard to read because
  each realm row carried two offset markers, two range lines, and a
  dashed/solid distinction simultaneously; separating the two metrics into
  their own row removes that.
- Fig 1b: gain, loss and net change are derived from the exact same
  per-GCM realm net changes used by Fig 3. Bars/marker show five-GCM means
  and all whiskers show the corresponding five-GCM min-max ranges.
- Fig 1d: grouped (not stacked) bars, with explicit xlim padding so value
  labels never collide with y-tick labels.
- Fig 4: GCM sign-agreement hatching at 3-of-5 (60%) threshold.
- S5: per-pixel GCM standard deviation of area change, companion to Fig 1a.
  Requires per-GCM timmean files (see open_timmean_da_gcm / the separate
  generate_gcm_timmean.py script).
- S8: per biome-realm, number of GCMs agreeing on sign, companion to Fig 4.
  Color scale now only spans the achievable range (3, 4, 5 out of 5) --
  with N=5 GCMs a majority can never be below 3/5, so 0/1/2 are impossible
  and were wasting half the color range.
- S9: GCM-resolved, error-barred version of Fig 1b/1d, kept separate from
  the main figure to avoid crowding it.
- S10 (rebuilt): per-GCM, per-biome-realm area table with both Total GDW
  and GDW-without-landuse-impact columns side by side, for every GCM,
  scenario, and biome-realm. This is the individual-model breakdown behind
  Fig 3 and Fig 4.
"""
# Numerical note:
# Pixel-level gross gain/loss/net calculations are summed in float64.
# Consistency checks use rtol=1e-6 and atol=1e-4 in units of 10^3 km²
# (0.1 km² absolute tolerance), preventing harmless floating-point rounding
# differences from stopping the script while still catching real mismatches.


